A||N||3||bcwawright||Home Made Cold Air Intake||||5||
Z||000000||bcwawright||05-20-2001||12:13 AM||||I was wanting to build a cold air intake...the ones I've seen on The Fiero Store website cost big bucks and it looks pretty simple.<BR>Does any one know where you can get 3" aluminum round thin wall tubing....can a muffler shop bend this with a nice radius curve(I think they call this mandrel bending where the bent tube has no buckling or crimping)......then does someone sell just the conical shaped clampon filter?<BR>Anyone have any suggestions or tips?<BR>Thanks in advance.<P>Bruce<BR>88GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||Nashco||05-20-2001||12:30 AM||||Use the search function.  This topic was worn out about a month ago, and about a month before that...there is a ton of info in those old posts.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||GoCartGuy||05-20-2001||12:31 AM||||Ok the tubing is going to be the hard part to find, best bet might be hit up a muffler shop or look for a wrecked semi, if I remember right the vent stacks for exhaust is three inch in diameter.  As far as the filter goes, easiest thing for a good cold air system is to purchase one with a heat sheild and clamp a section of dryer vent piping to the front of it.  The other end of the vent piping can be attatched to the intake from your side vents.  Most performance shops carry the heat sheilded cone filters.  Run ya about $40.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||05-20-2001||02:20 AM||||Check out sjp777's site. He has instructions on how he built his and you can tell from the pictures on his site that it looks great!<P> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
